{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790353400,"nonce":"cPxR2jJvyGeHGmi41aVOoJIIjBk=","title":"Swarm bench fails to open: `bench:Math` names a module of both `haxe` and `math`","message":"`cargo bench -p caribou-interop --bench swarm` panics at `Session::open` with \"publishing Math: `bench:Math` names a module of both `haxe` and `math`\".\n\nTwo things meet:\n\n- tests/plugin_session.rs copies the math plugin into crates/caribou-interop/fixtures/plugins/ and leaves it there. Every later session over the fixtures (the swarm and interop benches, other fixture programs) then loads the math plugin, which none of them asked for; the directory is gitignored, so nothing shows it.\n- The math plugin's free functions land on a class named after the plugin, module `Math`, and a plugin's language joins every namespace. The swarm arena uses Haxe's own `Math`, which the Haxe adapter publishes as module `Math` in `bench`, so the two languages claim `bench:Math`.\n\nThe first is test hygiene: the test should use a directory of its own. The second would hit any project with a plugin whose free-function class shares a name with a Haxe standard class (Math, Std, String) or a module of the project, so it wants a rule: which language wins a name in a namespace, or plugins keeping to their own namespace.\n\nMoving the copy aside lets the bench run.","files":null}]}